592186045417] [17592186050300 #edb/ref 1033 2205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050300 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3dfc702e-510d-534f-98bc-06c367122acf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1031 "Prime and Composite Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1033 840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1034 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "70dfa99f-c825-5da5-8721-f8fa26795d6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1031 "The Intersection of Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1033 2827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7c048599-260c-5867-88ec-692aa4d19c71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054363 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12675" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054363 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n\n- $P$: Compute the product of the first four prime numbers.\n\n- $Q$: Four is a prime number.\n\n- $R$: Is four a prime number?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054363 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db1f7851-43e9-5fee-9553-f9d6ead38b40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054363 #edb/ref 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options:\n\n- $P$ is *not* a statement because it is neither true nor false. Rather, it is an instruction.\n\n- $Q$ is a statement. This statement is *false* because $4$ is not a prime number (it is divisible by $2$).\n\n- $R$ is *not* a statement because it is neither true nor false. Rather, it is a question, and the possible answers are \"yes\" or \"no.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054364 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054364 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P:$ $4$ is a positive number.\n\n- $Q:$ $2^3$ is a natural number.\n\n- $R:$ $-4 \\geq 2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8ca6e60c-1411-5f6e-a9f4-d6209f847ddf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054364 #edb/ref 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate the truth value of each statement:\n\n- Statement $P$ is *true.* Indeed, $4$ is a positive number.\n\n- Statement $Q$ is *true.* Indeed,  $2^3=8$ is a natural number.\n\n- Statement $R$ is *false* because $-4 \\ngeq 2$ .\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054367 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054367 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n\n- $P: \\{5,6,7\\}\\cap \\{6,8,10\\} = \\{6\\}$\n\n- $Q: \\sqrt{3} +2$\n\n- $R: 4\\mid 2$\n\n**Note**: The notation \n\n$$\na\\mid b\n$$\n\nmeans \"$a$ divides $b.$\" This is equivalent to the statement that $b$ is divisible by $a.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054367 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d85fd26e-50f6-58f1-9e44-8df23863ee6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054367 #edb/ref 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options:\n\n- $P$ is a statement. This statement is *true* because the intersection of the given sets is indeed the set $\\{6\\}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\n- $R$ is a statement. This statement is *false* because $4$ does not divide $2$ (i.e., $2$ is not divisible by $4$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054451 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054451 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n\n- Subtract $x$ from $10.$\n\n- $y^2 -2  \\leq 3$\n\n- $1+z$ is a perfect square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054451 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a70765b4-08b9-5ebf-92f4-1b7c5266523e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054451 #edb/ref 1074 "A *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false. Rather, it gives an instruction.\n\n- Option II is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $z$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054817 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054817 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054817 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Truth Value of a Statement" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054817 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186054364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054817 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5f185774-4d55-515c-9032-8f6832c6999a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054818 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Mathematical Statements" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054818 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186054363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054818 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "228b0a63-bf0e-57bf-aa1e-ef5518236462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054819 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054819 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054819 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054819 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054819 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054819 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054819 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054819 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054819 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054819 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Statements Expressed Using Symbols" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054819 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186054367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054819 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4eda1965-d655-5550-9bb5-13e7a9c1b406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054820 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054820 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186054451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054820 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "35eba041-5109-5855-b132-174c0271c168" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054913 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054913 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054913 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054913 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054913 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054913 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054913 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054913 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054913 #edb/ref 1045 "Statements and Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054913 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186055855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054913 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054913 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b9939b6d-b334-5155-b887-f965a9871210" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460669 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186055034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460670 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186055034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460671 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186055034 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055034 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055034 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n- **P.** $P:2\\,|\\,4$\n- **Q.** $Q:\\sqrt{2}\\in N$\n- **R.** $R:2+2$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186055034 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a13c06f-6317-5f54-a60c-2244cd05e4c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055034 #edb/ref 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options:\n\n- $P$ is a statement. This statement is *true* because $2$ divides $4.$\n\n- $Q$ is a statement. This statement is *false* because $\\sqrt{2}$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $R$ is *not* a statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055035 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460672 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186055035 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460673 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186055035 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460674 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186055035 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170844" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055035 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055035 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n- **P.** $P:{1,2,3}\\cap {4,5,6}=∅$\n- **Q.** $Q:\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-{4}^{2}}$\n- **R.** $R:4$ is a prime number.\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186055035 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d26247f5-ea0b-58f7-ab8c-9e2873f56e29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055035 #edb/ref 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options:\n\n- $P$ is a statement. This statement is *true* because the intersection of the given sets is empty.\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\n- $R$ is a statement. This statement is *false* since $4$ is not prime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055044 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460675 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186055044 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460676 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186055044 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460677 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186055044 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055044 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055044 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n- **I.** $P(n):$ $n$ is even.\n- **II.** $Q(x):$ ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 1$\n- **III.** $R(y):$ Draw a line of width $y\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186055044 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c5327827-bdd3-59f2-8ac3-6f2fcb1af21e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055044 #edb/ref 1074 "A *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false. Rather, it will give an instruction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055595 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055595 #edb/ref 1061 161822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055595 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e2dd3491-1e68-563f-850e-0f1a50254eee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055595 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055855 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055855 #edb/ref 1061 64628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055855 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9dddcb7-93e7-5b0f-b0ab-3bede7e5408e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055855 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055856 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055856 #edb/ref 1061 64629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055856 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0fa5c65e-9772-56d9-b502-47729dbd3c60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055856 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055857 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055857 #edb/ref 1061 64632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055857 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e186ecb-bb2b-515b-9277-15fca8231263" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055857 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055858 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055858 #edb/ref 1061 64636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055858 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7738221b-b210-55a0-a14d-cb5a8da07c81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055858 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055859 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055859 #edb/ref 1061 64639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055859 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fe5e6113-0b7b-58a9-9078-465727b42948" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055859 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055860 #edb/ref 1061 64640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055860 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "da67dd80-78c6-52d8-867f-2aa94c264733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055860 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056004 #edb/ref 1064 "A **mathematical statement** is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either **true** or **false**.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The sentence, \"The sum of $1$ and $2$ is $3$\" is a mathematical statement because it is *true.*\n\n- Likewise, \"$2+2=5$\" is a mathematical statement because it is *false.*\n\nThe following examples are *not* mathematical statements:\n\n- \"Find the sum of $1$ and $2$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false. It is an instruction.\n\n- \"What is the sum of $1$ and $2?$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false. It is a question.\n\n- Similarly, \"$6+9$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\nWe often use upper-case letters such as $P, Q,$ and $R$ to represent statements. For example:\n\n- $P:$ The sum of two even integers is odd.\n\n- $Q: 3 \\in \\mathbb Z$\n\n- $R: \\{2,4,6\\ldots\\}\\cup \\{1,3,5,\\ldots\\} = \\mathbb N$\n\nIn this case, the statements $Q$ and $R$ are true, whereas statement $P$ is false.\n\nWe often refer to the **truth value** of a mathematical statement. We use [math]T[/math] to denote \"true\" and [math]F[/math] to denote \"false.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056004 #edb/ref 1065 7961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056004 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "27451355-1bea-50f9-8b12-71af26b849e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056004 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056051 #edb/ref 1064 "A **predicate** (or **open sentence**) of one variable is a sentence that contains a variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute a concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a mathematical statement that is either *true* or *false*.\n\nFor example, consider the following predicate:\n\n> $x > 1$\n\nThis is a predicate because substituting concrete values for $x$ gives a mathematical statement.\n\nFor instance: \n\n- Substituting $x=2$ gives the *true* statement \"$2 > 1$\"\n\n- Substituting $x=5$ gives the *true* statement \"$5 > 1$\"\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ gives the *false* statement \"$0 > 1$\"\n\nWe denote predicates using capital letters and the corresponding variable in parentheses. In this example, we can write our predicate more formally as follows:\n\n> $P(x):$ $x > 1$\n\nAny predicate must be defined with its **universal set,** which is the set from which we are allowed to pull the concrete values of the variable. So, we could state our predicate more formally as\n\n> $P(x):$ $x > 1,$ $\\quad$ where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nThe universal set can often be identified from the context and doesn't need to be explicitly stated. For example, if we have the predicate \n\n> $n$ is divisible by 5,\n\nwe can assume that $n$ is defined on the set of all integers, i.e., $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$\n\nIn math, *any equation or inequality is a predicate*. However, a sentence containing a variable is not necessarily a predicate. For example, the sentence \n\n$\\qquad$ \"What is the sign of $y?$\"\n\nis *not* a predicate because substituting a concrete value for $y$ does not give a mathematical statement. Instead, it gives a question. \n\nSimilarly, \n\n> $z^2-5$\n\nis *not* a predicate either. It's just an expression." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056051 #edb/ref 1065 8303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056051 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6162ecd9-50ee-52a9-9651-ba95a586ee9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056051 #edb/ref 1067 "Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056074 #edb/ref 1064 "Before we continue, we need a brief discussion on the concept of **integer divisibility.**\n\nSuppose $a$ and $b$ are integers. We write $a  \\mid b$ to mean that $\\boldsymbol a$ **divides** $\\boldsymbol b.$ Informally, this means the quotient of $\\dfrac b a$ is an *integer*, and there is *no remainder*. \n\nFor example, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n5 \\,|\\, 20\n$$\n\nThis statement reads, \"$5$ divides $20$\". Moreover, this is a *true* statement because $20\\div 5 = 4$ is an integer. \n\nNow consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n6\\,  |\\, 20\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false* because $20\\div 6 = 3 {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac13}},$ which is *not* an integer. The correct statement is\n\n$$\n6 \\! \\not | \\: 20.\n$$\n\nThis statement reads, \"$6$ does not divide $20$\".\n\nFor now, this is all we need to understand the notation $a\\mid b.$ More formal discussions on integer divisibility will be given in future lessons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056074 #edb/ref 1065 9348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056074 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ac10b43c-74e8-5752-9d06-2d746c31cb0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056074 #edb/ref 1067 "Divisibility Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284735 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466660 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186284735 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466661 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186284735 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466662 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186284735 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284735 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284735 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n- **I.** The square root of $x.$\n- **II.** $y$ is a composite number.\n- **III.** $z+3$ is equal to $8.$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186284735 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33aeb9a4-e17a-5f7f-8d45-99fe2421efca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284735 #edb/ref 1074 "A *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $z$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284737 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466666 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186284737 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466667 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186284737 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466668 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186284737 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284737 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284737 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n- **I.** $P(z):$ Subtract $8$ from $z.$\n- **II.** $Q(x):$ $x÷2$\n- **III.** $R(n):$ $\\sqrt{n}-\\sqrt{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186284737 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "388254b7-705e-581e-9708-313da2ad5c8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284737 #edb/ref 1074 "A *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $z$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false. Rather, it will give an instruction.\n\n- Option II is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the given statements.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352417 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\pi $ is an irrational number." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352417 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352417 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5fa39ce5-4b78-55ec-9f01-4f7bd59537c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352558 #edb/ref 1080 "The negative numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352558 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352558 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "70d9bcf1-e3eb-5236-88ab-e95db6fbda70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352798 #edb/ref 1080 "The set of the natural numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352798 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352798 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8d307d6b-3d62-5aee-bdbf-9bebf76eb0a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352912 #edb/ref 1080 "The height of a triangle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352912 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352912 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9d7a6ee3-febb-5d40-9985-6bc10b5e3386" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353344 #edb/ref 1080 "Subtract $2$ from $5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353344 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353344 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cca54673-b9a2-585f-a846-9648ba9e245e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353885 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353885 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353885 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186352417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353885 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "295d4088-ac28-5602-a22f-48b75fc37e32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354472 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354472 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86473" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354472 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354472 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a mathematical statement?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354472 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87061480-a114-5f8d-a323-725d4db909c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354472 #edb/ref 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false. \n\nOf the given sentences, the only sentence that is either true or false is \"$\\pi $ is an irrational number.\" (This sentence is true.)\n\nAll the other sentences are neither true nor false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472092 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472093 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472094 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174419" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378988 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical statements?\n- **P.** $P:(-2)\\cdot 3$\n- **Q.** $Q:5>3$\n- **R.** $R:7\\in N$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66e732f7-06d5-591e-98a5-b6812f6bf5f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378988 #edb/ref 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options:\n\n- $P$ is *not* a statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\n- $Q$ is a statement. This statement is *true* because $5$ is larger than  $3.$\n\n- $R$ is a statement. This statement is *true* because $7$ is a natural number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ and $R$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399364 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399364 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a mathematical statement?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b625ee5e-9a08-5485-b20f-479dacadfb89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399364 #edb/ref 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false. \n\nOf the given sentences, the only sentence that is either true or false is \"$1$ is a rational number.\" (This sentence is true.)\n\nAll the other sentences are neither true nor false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399365 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399365 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399365 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399365 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399365 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399365 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399365 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399365 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186399369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399365 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "87c7d492-3fd1-55b7-9667-7d1d03627d97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399366 #edb/ref 1080 "Compute the sum of the first two natural numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399366 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399366 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5d281e4c-12f3-5079-93f5-be66bce4c00b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399367 #edb/ref 1080 "The set of rational numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399367 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399367 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4fc90903-d3c5-5540-9c56-a82ad70a2ac1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399368 #edb/ref 1080 "The diagonal of a quadrilateral." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399368 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399368 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e4002685-8407-5008-9596-c51979ab7920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399369 #edb/ref 1080 "$1$ is a rational number." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399369 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399369 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cbdda3a7-d2ed-58bf-a55c-bd1037a65ec2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399370 #edb/ref 1080 "What is a prime number?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399370 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399370 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4a90792e-413a-5402-b9c4-94db6437b94a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186459605 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186459605 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186459605 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3ea52b5b-53d4-5